7. sizeof() Operator
sizeof() is an operator (not a function) which returns the number of bytes required for a given data type or variable.
c// Example
printf("%d", sizeof(char));    // 1
printf("%d", sizeof(short));   // 2
printf("%d", sizeof(int));     // 4
printf("%d", sizeof(long));    // 4 (32-bit) or 8 (64-bit)
printf("%d", sizeof(3.10));    // 4 (or 8 for double)

Note: sizeof expressions are not allowed inside sizeof().

cvoid main() {
    short int a = 10;
    printf("%d", sizeof(a));         // 2
    printf("%d", sizeof(a=30));      // 2  (expression allowed but not evaluated)
}
// O/P: 2  10

8. Type Promotionality Rule
When performing any operation with 2 arguments of different types, the compiler will:

Promote the lower type value temporarily to the higher type
Perform the operation
Return the result in the higher type

signed(int) → always positive result
sizeof(int) = signed
→ compiler will promote both args to higher type and perform the operation
Example
cvoid main() {
    signed int S = -3;
    unsigned int U = 3;
    if(S > U)
        printf("Hello");
    else
        printf("Hai");
    // O/P: Hello   (due to type promotionality)
}

Explanation: signed int gets promoted to unsigned int. -3 as unsigned becomes a very large number → greater than 3 → prints "Hello".
